@database "file_ops"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: file_ops.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "File operations"
@{h1}File operations

The tools an agent works on files with: read a text file, list a folder,
find files by name, search their text, find code by meaning, and -- unless
the instance is read-only -- create, replace, edit, delete, move and rename
files. Every path must lie inside the folders the instance is given; the
plugin refuses everything else before it touches the disk. Several agents
use it under different instance names, each with folders of its own.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Where an agent can read and write  " link sandbox}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Reading and listing  " link read}
  @{"  Finding files and text  " link search}
  @{"  Semantic search  " link semantic}
  @{"  Changing files  " link write}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node sandbox "Where an agent can read and write"
@{h1}Where an agent can read and write

@{h2}Instances, folders and the allowlist

The plugin runs as instances: each server entry with @{tt}type: file_ops@{utt}
in the configuration is one, with its own name, its own folders and its own
tools. The tools are named after the instance --
@{tt}file_ops_read_file@{utt}, @{tt}coder_fs_read_file@{utt},
@{tt}coder_fs_ro_read_file@{utt} -- and an agent gets them through its
allowlist, @{tt}+coder_fs/*@{utt} for all tools of @{tt}coder_fs@{utt}. What
an agent can reach is therefore decided in two places: its allowlist says
which instances it has, and each instance's @{tt}allowed_directories@{utt}
say which folders those instances reach.

@{table}
Instance | Folders | Used by
file_ops | data/workspace | the default instance, e.g. the sysadmin agent
coder_fs | data/workspace/, src/plugins/coder/skills/, src/, . | the coder, read and write
coder_fs_ro | the same, read-only | the coder's explorer, reviewer and tester
workspace_file_ops | . (off) | nobody; see "Setting it up"
@{body}

A read-only instance (@{tt}read_only: true@{utt}) does not offer the write
tools at all, and refuses them if one is called anyway -- so "the reviewer
only reads" is a property of the configuration, not a sentence in a prompt.

@{h2}What an entry in allowed_directories means

@{bullet}A relative entry (@{tt}src@{utt}, @{tt}data/workspace/@{utt})
counts from the project folder.
@{bullet}@{tt}.@{utt} (also @{tt}./@{utt}) is the folder the command was
started in -- where you stood when you typed @{tt}agent-cli@{utt}. Started
in the project, that is the project. With @{tt}.@{utt} in the list, the
agent can reach everything below that folder, the configuration and its
secrets included when it is the project.
@{bullet}A @{tt}data/...@{utt} entry follows the data directory when that
is moved (@{tt}AGENT_DATA_DIR@{utt}, @{tt}paths.data_dir@{utt}).
@{bullet}Without the key, as with an empty list, the instance allows
nothing: every path is refused. (It used to get @{tt}src@{utt},
@{tt}docs@{utt}, @{tt}tests@{utt} and @{tt}tmp@{utt} of the project,
writable -- enough for an agent to plant a plugin the next start loads.)

The folders are fixed when the instance starts; a later change of the
working directory does not move them.

@{h2}How a path is checked

Every path a tool takes goes through the same check, before anything on the
disk is opened:

@{bullet}A relative path counts from the folder the server runs in, the
project -- also when the
allowed folder is @{tt}.@{utt} and you started elsewhere; there an agent has
to use absolute paths.
@{bullet}The path is resolved first -- @{tt}..@{utt} collapsed, links and
junctions followed -- and then it must lie inside one of the folders.
@{tt}notes/../notes/a.md@{utt} is allowed, @{tt}../outside.txt@{utt} is
not, whatever it is spelt like. A link inside a folder that points outside
is refused for reading and writing. A hard link is not: it is the file
itself, not a pointer to it, so a hard link made inside a folder to a file
outside reads that file. Making one needs write access to the folder by
other means, a terminal for instance; this plugin cannot. Writing to it
replaces the entry and leaves the outside file as it was.
@{bullet}An absolute path is fine when it lands inside, on any drive; one on
another drive or elsewhere on this one is refused. On Windows
@{tt}/e/Projects/x@{utt}, as Git Bash writes it, means
@{tt}E:\\Projects\\x@{utt}.
@{bullet}A network or device path -- @{tt}\\\\host\\share\\x@{utt},
@{tt}//host/share/x@{utt}, @{tt}\\\\?\\UNC\\...@{utt},
@{tt}\\\\.\\pipe\\...@{utt} -- is refused on its text alone, unless it lies
inside a folder that is itself such a share. That covers every path that
begins with two slashes or backslashes in any mix, and the NT prefix
@{tt}\\??\\@{utt} (also written @{tt}/??/@{utt}), as in
@{tt}\\??\\UNC\\host\\share@{utt}. Resolving such a path first would
make Windows connect to that host and sign in with your credentials (an
NTLM hash to whoever runs it); refused on its text, the host is never
contacted. The check is the core's, shared by every plugin that uses its
path sandbox.
@{bullet}A path starting with @{tt}~@{utt} is refused: it is not your home
folder here, and would otherwise become a folder named @{tt}~@{utt}.
@{bullet}A path with a NUL character is refused.

Paths an agent never types are guarded too. A search only walks the
allowed folders and never enters a linked folder or a junction; a linked
file is found only when its target lies inside an allowed folder.

@{h2}What an agent cannot do inside

@{bullet}Delete, move or rename an allowed folder itself -- only what is in
it.
@{bullet}Reach through a link: deleting, moving or renaming a link acts on
the link, never on what it points to; a junction counts as a link. A link
or a folder moves only within one drive -- across drives the move is
refused rather than copied, because a copy would bring in what a junction
points to. (Creating or editing a file through a link inside the folders
writes the file it points to, as an editor would.)

@{h2}What the check does not cover

@{bullet}Windows device names are not refused by name. On Windows 11
@{tt}CON@{utt}, @{tt}COM1@{utt}, @{tt}con.txt@{utt} or @{tt}AUX.log@{utt}
are ordinary file names; only @{tt}NUL@{utt} still names the null device,
which exists but is not a file, so reading it fails.
@{bullet}The folders bound this plugin only. A shell tool given to the same
agent reaches whatever the shell reaches.
@endnode

@node read "Reading and listing"
@{h1}Reading and listing

@{h2}read_file

Reads a text file. Always UTF-8; bytes that are not are replaced, so a
binary file comes back as unreadable text rather than an error.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
filePath | the file
offset | the first line, counted from 1 (0 means 1 too)
limit | how many lines
@{body}

Without @{tt}offset@{utt} and @{tt}limit@{utt} a file up to
@{tt}max_unpaginated_file_size_kb@{utt} (100 KB) comes back whole, with its
own line endings; a larger one comes back as its first
@{tt}default_line_limit@{utt} (500) lines. With an offset and no limit, a
small file is read to its end, a large one for 500 lines.

The answer: @{tt}content@{utt}, @{tt}total_lines@{utt},
@{tt}lines_read@{utt}, @{tt}offset@{utt} (here counted from 0),
@{tt}encoding@{utt}, @{tt}file_path@{utt}. Refusals are
@{tt}{"status": "error", "error": ..., "error_type": ...}@{utt} with
@{tt}FileNotFoundError@{utt} ("File not found: ..."),
@{tt}NotAFileError@{utt}, @{tt}SecurityError@{utt} (outside the folders)
or @{tt}ValueError@{utt} for a number it cannot read ("offset: a whole
number, got 'x'"). Numbers sent as text -- @{tt}"40"@{utt} -- are taken.

@{h2}list_directory

@{table}
Parameter | Default | Meaning
dir_path | -- | the folder
recursive | false | the whole tree below it
pattern | -- | a glob the entries must match, see @{" Finding files and text " link search}
include_hidden | false | also names starting with a dot
include_ignored | false | also what .gitignore and the exclude patterns hide, and dotfiles
max_results | 200 | the most entries, files and folders together
@{body}

A plain listing shows the folder's own entries. A recursive one uses the
same walk as the searches: it skips what @{tt}.gitignore@{utt} and the
exclude patterns hide, never enters a linked folder, and only lists a
linked file whose target lies in the listed folder.

The answer: @{tt}files@{utt} and @{tt}directories@{utt} as full paths,
sorted, their counts, @{tt}truncated@{utt} when there were more than
@{tt}max_results@{utt}, and @{tt}skipped@{utt}: which filters were on and
notes saying what that means for an empty answer.

@{code json}
{"filePath": "E:/work/app/main.py"}
{"filePath": "E:/work/app/big.log", "offset": 501, "limit": 500}
{"dir_path": "E:/work/app", "recursive": true, "pattern": "*.py"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node search "Finding files and text"
@{h1}Finding files and text

Both tools walk all allowed folders on demand -- no index, nothing to wait
for. Folders that lie inside each other are walked once.

@{h2}search_files

Finds files by a glob: @{tt}pattern@{utt}, @{tt}max_results@{utt} (50),
@{tt}include_ignored@{utt} (false). The answer: @{tt}files@{utt} (full
paths, sorted), @{tt}total_found@{utt}, @{tt}truncated@{utt},
@{tt}skipped@{utt}.

@{table}
Pattern | Matches
*.py | the file name, at any depth
hooks/*.py | a path below any folder called hooks; * stays within one folder
src/**/*.py | ** spans any number of folders, none included
test_?.py | ? is one character
[ab].txt | one of the listed characters
@{body}

Patterns ignore case. Backslashes, a leading @{tt}./@{utt} and a leading
part that repeats the allowed folder's own name
(@{tt}data/workspace/*.txt@{utt} when the folder is data/workspace) are
understood.

@{h2}grep_search

@{table}
Parameter | Default | Meaning
query | -- | the text, or with is_regex a Python regular expression
is_regex | false | treat query as a regular expression
case_sensitive | false | tell upper and lower case apart
include_pattern | -- | only files matching this glob
context_lines | 2 | lines before and after each match
max_results | 100 | the most matches
include_ignored | false | also what the filters hide
@{body}

Each match: @{tt}file_path@{utt}, @{tt}line_number@{utt} (from 1, as
read_file counts), @{tt}line_content@{utt}, @{tt}context_before@{utt},
@{tt}context_after@{utt}. Also @{tt}total_matches@{utt},
@{tt}total_files@{utt} (files actually read), @{tt}truncated@{utt},
@{tt}skipped@{utt}. Files over @{tt}max_file_size_for_indexing_kb@{utt}
(1024 KB) and binary files (decided by their first 4 KB, not their name)
are skipped and counted in the notes. A broken regular expression answers
@{tt}RegexError@{utt}.

@{h2}What is skipped

@{bullet}The exclude patterns: @{tt}.git@{utt}, @{tt}__pycache__@{utt},
@{tt}node_modules@{utt}, @{tt}.venv@{utt}, @{tt}*.pyc@{utt},
@{tt}*.min.js@{utt}, @{tt}*.min.css@{utt}, @{tt}*.map@{utt} -- or the
instance's @{tt}search.exclude_patterns@{utt}, which replace them.
@{bullet}What each allowed folder's @{tt}.gitignore@{utt} and
@{tt}.ignore@{utt} hide (needs the @{tt}pathspec@{utt} package).
@{bullet}Names starting with a dot, unless @{tt}search.search_hidden@{utt}.

@{tt}include_ignored: true@{utt} lifts all of them except @{tt}.git@{utt}
-- on a large tree that walk takes minutes. An allowed folder that lies
inside another one is never hidden by the outer one's filters. When a
search finds nothing while filters were on, its notes say so: "not found"
and "hidden" are different answers.

@{code json}
{"pattern": "**/test_*.py"}
{"query": "def cancel", "include_pattern": "*.py", "context_lines": 1}
{"query": "TODO|FIXME", "is_regex": true, "max_results": 20}
@{body}
@endnode

@node semantic "Semantic search"
@{h1}Semantic search

Finds code by meaning when the agent does not know the word the code uses:
"how a run is cancelled" finds the cancel handler. It is off unless an
instance sets @{tt}search.enable_semantic_search: true@{utt}; otherwise the
tool answers @{tt}SemanticSearchDisabled@{utt} and names grep_search.

@{table}
Parameter | Default | Meaning
query | -- | what to look for, in words
max_results | 10 | how many hits
filter_pattern | -- | a glob on the file's path or name, e.g. *.py
@{body}

Each hit is a symbol -- a Python function or class, a Markdown heading, or
a short window of other text -- with @{tt}file_path@{utt},
@{tt}line@{utt}, @{tt}symbol@{utt}, @{tt}similarity_score@{utt} (1 is the
same meaning), @{tt}distance@{utt}, @{tt}filename@{utt},
@{tt}size_bytes@{utt} and @{tt}extension@{utt}. With a filter the search
looks ten times deeper (at most 200) before filtering, and says when none
of those matched it.

@{h2}The index

The first semantic search of an instance starts building the index in the
background and answers @{tt}IndexNotReady@{utt} until it is done -- it never
waits for it. An instance that builds the index answers from it only once a
whole pass has finished, never "nothing found" from half an index. An
instance that only reads a shared index (@{tt}enable_indexing: false@{utt},
like @{tt}coder_fs_ro@{utt}) cannot know that: it answers as soon as the
store holds anything, so while the owner's first build runs its hits cover
only part of the tree. After
that, the index is refreshed every @{tt}auto_reindex_interval@{utt} seconds
(300), and a search picks up what changed since, at most every 30 seconds,
without waiting for it. The embeddings are made locally; nothing leaves the
machine. The index lives in @{tt}data/cache/file_ops_chromadb@{utt}, one
collection per instance (@{tt}file_ops_<instance>@{utt}). Two instances on
the same tree can share one: give both the same
@{tt}collection_name@{utt} and set @{tt}enable_indexing: false@{utt} on the
one that only reads -- as @{tt}coder_fs_ro@{utt} does with
@{tt}coder_fs@{utt}'s index.
@endnode

@node write "Changing files"
@{h1}Changing files

Only an instance that is not read-only has these tools; a read-only one
answers @{tt}ReadOnlyError@{utt} if they are called anyway.

@{h2}manage

@{table}
operation | Needs | Does
create | path, content | writes a new file; with overwrite: true replaces one whole
delete | path | removes a file or an empty folder; a full one with recursive: true
move | path, destination | moves a file or folder; the destination must not exist
rename | path, new_name | renames in place; new_name has no / or \\
@{body}

@{bullet}create makes missing parent folders, writes through a temporary
file next to the target and renames it into place, so a file is never
half-written; a replaced file keeps its permissions. Answer:
@{tt}file_path@{utt}, @{tt}bytes_written@{utt}, @{tt}created_dirs@{utt},
@{tt}replaced@{utt}. An existing file without overwrite answers
@{tt}FileExistsError@{utt}; a folder, @{tt}IsADirectoryError@{utt}.
@{bullet}delete answers the @{tt}type@{utt} it removed: file, directory or
link. A link goes, what it points to stays. A full folder without
recursive answers @{tt}DirectoryNotEmptyError@{utt}.
@{bullet}move creates the destination's parent folders. A file also moves
to another drive; a link or a folder only within one, see
@{" Where an agent can read and write " link sandbox}.
@{bullet}An allowed folder itself cannot be deleted, moved or renamed.
@{bullet}@{tt}overwrite@{utt} and @{tt}recursive@{utt} take true/false,
also as text: @{tt}"false"@{utt} is false. Anything else is refused with a
message naming the parameter.

@{h2}replace_string_in_file

Replaces one piece of text in a file: @{tt}filePath@{utt},
@{tt}oldString@{utt}, @{tt}newString@{utt}. The old text must occur
exactly once.

@{bullet}Found once: replaced. The file keeps its line endings and
permissions, written the same safe way as create. Answer:
@{tt}changes.replacements@{utt} (1) and @{tt}changes.lines@{utt}, the first
and last line the new text now stands on.
@{bullet}The model's @{tt}\\n@{utt} matches a file's @{tt}\\r\\n@{utt}.
@{bullet}Found more than once: @{tt}AmbiguousMatchError@{utt} with
@{tt}occurrences@{utt} -- nothing is changed; add surrounding lines.
@{bullet}Found only with different indentation:
@{tt}WhitespaceMatchError@{utt} with the file's line, the searched line
and a @{tt}corrected_old_string@{utt} to send instead.
@{bullet}Not found: @{tt}StringNotFoundError@{utt}.

@{code json}
{"operation": "create", "path": "E:/work/app/notes.md", "content": "# Notes\n"}
{"operation": "move", "path": "E:/work/app/notes.md", "destination": "E:/work/app/docs/notes.md"}
{"filePath": "E:/work/app/main.py", "oldString": "retries = 3\n", "newString": "retries = 5\n"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

Seven tools, named @{tt}<instance>_read_file@{utt},
@{tt}_manage@{utt}, @{tt}_replace_string_in_file@{utt},
@{tt}_list_directory@{utt}, @{tt}_search_files@{utt},
@{tt}_grep_search@{utt} and @{tt}_semantic_search@{utt}; a read-only
instance leaves out manage and replace_string_in_file. Their descriptions
come from the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}; read_file's names the size
and line limits of its instance. The bounds the schema shows are held by
the server: a number above one is taken as the maximum, below as the
minimum -- @{tt}max_results@{utt} 1 to 1000 for list_directory, 1 to 500
for search_files and grep_search, 1 to 50 for semantic_search,
@{tt}context_lines@{utt} 0 to 10, read_file's @{tt}limit@{utt} at least 1. A refusal outside the folders reads "Path
is outside the allowed directories: X. Allowed: A, B" -- the model learns
where it may work.

The chat's status line ends with the result: "Read main.py: 120/120
lines", "Created notes.md: 9 bytes", "Replaced in main.py: 1
occurrence(s)", "Deleted file: old.txt", "Listed app: 12 files, 3
directories", "Grep 'def cancel': 4 matches in 2 files", "File search
'*.py': 50 matches".

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Tool answers are ordinary tool results: appended, nothing earlier changes.
The costly ones are the reads: read_file returns a file up to 100 KB
whole, and grep_search returns each matching line with its context. On a
large tree narrow the pattern and keep @{tt}max_results@{utt} small.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Relative paths count from the project, also when the allowed
folder is the launch folder.
@{bullet}There is no size limit on reading or writing a file; only
read_file's pages and grep's skipping of large files bound what an answer
holds.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The default instance, in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
file_ops:
  type: file_ops
  enabled: true
  allowed_directories:
    - data/workspace
  max_unpaginated_file_size_kb: 100  # Auto-paginate files larger than 100KB
  default_line_limit: 500  # Default lines when paginating large files
  search:
    enable_indexing: true
    index_on_startup: true
    enable_semantic_search: true
    auto_reindex_interval: 3600
    exclude_patterns:
      - "**/.git/**"
      - "**/__pycache__/**"
      - "**/node_modules/**"
      - "**/.venv/**"
      - "**/*.min.js"
      - "**/*.min.css"
      - "**/*.map"
@{body}

A second instance is a second entry with @{tt}type: file_ops@{utt} and a
name of its own. The coder's read-only twin, from
@{tt}src/plugins/coder/agents/tools.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
coder_fs_ro:
  type: file_ops
  enabled: true
  description: "coder harness: read-only file access for reviewer and explorer"
  read_only: true
  allowed_directories:
    - data/workspace/
    - src/plugins/coder/skills/
    - src/
    - .
  max_unpaginated_file_size_kb: 200
  default_line_limit: 500
  search:
    enable_semantic_search: true
    enable_indexing: false
    collection_name: file_ops_coder_tree
    exclude_patterns:
      - '**/.git/**'
      - '**/__pycache__/**'
      - '**/node_modules/**'
      - '**/.venv/**'
      - '**/*.pyc'
      - '**/dist/**'
      - '**/build/**'
@{body}

An agent sees an instance's tools when its allowlist names it:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+coder_fs_ro/*"
@{body}

@{tt}workspace_file_ops@{utt}, also in plugins.yaml, allows @{tt}.@{utt}
and is off: it reaches the whole checkout -- secrets, the user database,
every session. Switch it on only for an agent you trust with all of that.

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
allowed_directories | none: nothing allowed | the folders; see "Where an agent can read and write"
read_only | false | true: no write tools
max_unpaginated_file_size_kb | 100 | read_file returns a file up to this size whole
default_line_limit | 500 | lines read_file returns of a larger file without limit
@{body}

Under @{tt}search:@{utt}

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
exclude_patterns | .git, __pycache__, node_modules, .venv, *.pyc, *.min.js, *.min.css, *.map | globs every walk skips; a list here replaces them
search_hidden | false | also search dotfiles
max_file_size_for_indexing_kb | 1024 | larger files are not indexed and not searched by grep
enable_semantic_search | false | the semantic_search tool works
enable_indexing | true | this instance builds and refreshes the index; false: it only reads one
index_on_startup | true | the first semantic search starts the build; false: nothing ever does
auto_reindex_interval | 300 | seconds between refreshes of the index
collection_name | file_ops_<instance> | the index collection; the same name shares one
chroma_db_path | data/cache/file_ops_chromadb | where the index lives
force_recreate_chroma_collection | false | drop the index when the store opens
@{body}

It needs @{tt}pathspec@{utt} to honour @{tt}.gitignore@{utt}; without it
the walks skip only the exclude patterns and say so. Semantic search needs
a vector store (@{tt}chromadb@{utt}); the embedding model is downloaded
once on first use.

Each instance also tells other plugins which folders it works on: the
project-instructions plugin reads an @{tt}AGENTS.md@{utt} from them, and
the file checkpoints plugin takes them as the folders whose changes it
records.
@endnode
